<h2 id="sinkaudit">16 — What the Reference Actually Removes</h2>

<p>Every number on this page that says how much of the CFD cooling the replay captures is a ratio, and the bottom of that ratio is the cooling of the reference. That bottom had been taken on trust. It is worth measuring, because the replay copies the reference's own sink formula, so the two ought to be removing the same heat from the same cells.</p>

<p>They are not, and the difference is large enough to change how everything above should be read.</p>

<h3>16.1 — Measuring the lid term</h3>

<p>The heat-loss case and the adiabatic case share a mesh, an inlet schedule and a mass flow. They differ in one thing, the sink at the lid. Their fields are stored every 15 s, so each term of the heat balance can be read straight out of them: what is stored in the tank, what enters at the inlet, what leaves at the outlet, and what the lid takes.</p>

<p>Reading both cases and subtracting one balance from the other is better than reading either alone, because whatever bias the flux estimates carry is the same on both sides and cancels. The two inlet integrals agree to 1.3 J out of 25.3 kJ, which is the check that the pair really is a controlled one. What is left after the subtraction is the heat the lid took: <strong>6581 J</strong>.</p>

<p>The formula the case declares gives a different answer. Evaluated on the reference's own fields, $h A (T_\text{cell} - T_\infty)$ with $h = 100$ W/m²K over the 1125 lid cells integrates to <strong>1575 J</strong> over the same window. The reference removes <strong>4.18 times</strong> the heat its stated boundary condition accounts for.</p>

<p>Three further routes agree. The global balance of the heat-loss case on its own asks for 6763 J. A control volume drawn around the lid cell row, fed by advection and conduction from below, asks for 8586 J. And the replay, applying the declared formula to its own lid row, removes 1930 J. The replay is faithful to the boundary condition it was given. It is the reference that is not.</p>

<figure class="wide">
  <img src="assets/figures/fig_wall_sink_finding.png" alt="Heat leaving through the lid: the reference measured against its own formula, and the replay" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
  <figcaption><span class="label">Figure 20.</span> Left: the heat leaving through the lid. The reference's own formula (dashed) and the replay with the wall term as the case writes it sit together near 3.5 W, while the heat the reference actually loses (solid) runs about four times higher. The gain of &sect;16.5 puts the replay on the measured curve. Right: the mean temperature that follows. Without the gain the replay tracks the <em>adiabatic</em> reference far more closely than the heat-loss one.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3>16.2 — Why the gap looked like a limit</h3>

<p>This changes how the earlier sections read. A replay removing 1930 J was being scored against a reference removing 6581 J, so most of the distance between them was never a transport problem. It was a boundary condition the two sides did not share.</p>

<p>The shape of the error says the same. In the plain replay the upper layer ended 7.8 K too warm, and it was too warm almost <em>uniformly</em>: the difference map at the end of the run showed the whole region above the thermocline in one flat colour, at a mean absolute difference of 5.20 K. That is a level error, not a shape error. A sharpening step moves heat between neighbouring cells and changes the shape of a front. It cannot lower the temperature of a whole well-mixed layer, because inside that layer there is nowhere for the heat to go. That is not a matter of strength: the step is conservative by construction, taking the same amount from one cell that it gives to its neighbour, so its net effect on the heat in the tank is exactly zero. Only the wall term and the outlet can change that total.</p>

<p>This also explains the shape of the earlier conclusions. The capture ratio and the mean temperature are both dominated by the missing 4.7 kJ, which is the same in every run of the family. What varied between the operators was the small change each made to how much heat reached the outlet, and against a constant deficit that variation was the only thing the metric could see. So every form of the step appeared to cost cooling, and the family appeared to meet a ceiling. Both readings are correct as observations and wrong as explanations. So every form of the step was tried against an error it had no way of reaching, and the family looked as though it had met a ceiling.</p>

<p>The montages carried the same signature and were read the same way. Frame after frame showed a warm upper layer that no operator would shift, and the natural reading was that the recorded motion did not carry enough heat downward. Part of that reading survives, and it is the front. The larger part does not.</p>

<p>One earlier remark needs correcting in the light of this. Section 13 turned $h$ up and called the result <em>papering over the missing flow with an unphysical coefficient</em>. On the evidence above, raising the replay's coefficient is not papering over anything: it is setting the replay to remove the heat the reference actually removes. The physical objection was reasonable when the reference's own lid term had not been measured. It no longer holds.</p>

<h3>16.3 — What the extra removal is not</h3>

<p>The number invites an obvious question: where does the extra heat go? The source term was instrumented so it could answer for itself. It records the value it hands the solver into a user memory, and a hook sums that over the field at the end of every time step. Over a window inside the hot plateau it reports 3.44 W over exactly 1125 cells. That is the declared formula, over the intended cells. The source term is not saying one thing and doing another.</p>

<p>Six further candidates were then run rather than argued about, each as a short window restarted from the same stored field.</p>

<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Control</th><th class="num">Unaccounted removal (W)</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>As built</td><td class="num">+9.76</td></tr>
<tr><td>Source linearisation removed</td><td class="num">+9.66</td></tr>
<tr><td>Inner iterations raised from 30 to 100</td><td class="num">+9.63</td></tr>
<tr><td>Time step cut from 0.2 to 0.05 s</td><td class="num">+9.67</td></tr>
<tr><td>Outlet backflow</td><td class="num">none, 0 faces</td></tr>
<tr><td>Domain mass</td><td class="num">differs by 0.03%</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Coefficient set to zero (null control)</strong></td><td class="num"><strong>−1.16</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>Density held constant (buoyancy off)</td><td class="num">≈ 0.2 to 2</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<p>The null control is the one that matters most, because it tests the measurement rather than the case: with the coefficient set to zero the budget closes to −1.16 W. The method is sound, and the imbalance appears only when the sink is switched on. With the density held constant, so that the cooled lid can no longer drive any overturning, the imbalance largely goes with it.</p>

<p>The extra removal is therefore tied to the buoyancy-driven convection the sink sets off, and not to the sink's own value, its linearisation, the time step or the iteration count.</p>

<h3>16.4 &mdash; How the lid term is implemented</h3>

<p>The lid term is a few lines, and they are worth reading, because the rest of this section rests on what they do. The one thing they need first is the height of the top of the tank. An on-demand function walks the cells once before the run and reduces the largest cell-centre height over the partitions, which on this mesh is $y_\text{max} = 74.5$ mm.</p>

<p>The volumetric source, as the reference case is run. Fluent calls it for every cell of the fluid zone:</p>

<pre><code>DEFINE_SOURCE(top_heat_loss, c, t, dS, eqn)
{
    C_CENTROID(x, c, t);
    cs = pow(C_VOLUME(c, t), 1.0/3.0);        /* cell edge length */

    if(Top_region_initialized &amp;&amp; (x[1] &gt; (Top_y_max - cs))){

        source  = -H_EXT * (C_T(c, t) - T_AMB) / cs;   /* (W/m3) */
        dS[eqn] = -H_EXT / cs;
        return source;
    }
    dS[eqn] = 0.0;
    return 0.0;
}</code></pre>

<p>The test $x_1 &gt; y_\text{max} - c_s$ keeps the row whose centres are at 74.5 mm and drops the next row down at 73.5 mm, so the source acts on exactly 1125 cells. Dividing by $c_s$ turns the surface coefficient into a volumetric one: integrated over a cell of edge $c_s$ it is $h\,c_s^2\,(T-T_\text{amb})$, the convective loss across the cell's top face. <code>dS</code> is the derivative Fluent uses to linearise the term, and setting it to zero changes the imbalance by 0.1 W, which is one of the controls above.</p>

<h3>16.5 &mdash; A gain on the wall term</h3>

<p>The measurement above leaves one thing to do. The replay applies the coefficient the case carries and removes 1930 J; the reference removes 6581 J. The coefficient is not what is wrong, so it is left where it is and the correction is written as a factor of its own,</p>

<p style="text-align:center">$q_\text{cell} = h\,c_s^2\,(T_\text{cell} - T_\text{amb})\,G$</p>

<p>with $G = 4.35$. The value is not fitted to the temperature the replay should reach: it is set so that the two sides remove the same heat, 6623 J against 6581 J. Writing it this way rather than folding it into $h$ keeps the coefficient the case carries visible in the code and gives the correction a name, so a reader can see what was added and can set it back to one.</p>

<p>The interesting question is whether $G$ has to be a constant. The database stores, frame by frame and cell by cell, the mixing the CFD actually had, and the extra removal was shown above to be tied to the overturning the sink sets off. So the recording ought to know where the correction belongs. Writing</p>

<p style="text-align:center">$G = 1 + (G_0 - 1)\,D_\text{cell} / D_\text{ref}$</p>

<p>leaves one number, the level $G_0$, and takes the shape in space and time from the recording. That form can fail in a way the constant cannot, and the first version of it did: with $D_\text{ref}$ taken as the largest recorded value anywhere in the field, the weight at the lid is small, because the largest values live in the jet. The correction is then nearly absent exactly where it is needed. Taking $D_\text{ref}$ over the lid row instead asks the right question, which is how vigorously this piece of lid is being renewed compared with the rest of the lid.</p>

